tSlug": "louis-xii-of-france", "targetName": "Louis XII of France", "context": "Jean-François de la Trémoille acted as a trusted diplomat and advisor to King Louis XII of France in matters concerning the Papacy and Italian policy."}, {"sourceSlug": "house-of-la-trémoille", "sourceName": "House of La Trémoille", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "jean-françois-de-la-trémoille", "targetName": "Jean-François de la Trémoille", "context": "Jean-François de la Trémoille was a prominent member of the powerful and influential French aristocratic House of La Trémoille."}], "places": [{"name": "Auch, France", "role": "Archiepiscopal See"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Diplomatic Destination"}, {"name": "France", "role": "Homeland & Base"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jean-François de la Trémoille", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jean-françois-de-la-trémoille"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1088190", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois_de_la_Tr%C3%A9moille"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Jean-François de la Trémoille's career as a cardinal and diplomat was significant for strengthening French influence within the Papacy during the Italian Wars, reflecting the strategic intertwining of aristocratic power and ecclesiastical office. His actions helped to advance King Louis XII's foreign policy objectives in Italy.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-14T06:53:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29312614555", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jean-François de la Trémoille was a French Roman Catholic bishop and cardinal.", "newValue": "Jean-François de la Trémoille (died 1507), a scion of the powerful French House of La Trémoille, rose to prominence as a Roman Catholic cardinal and diplomat during a pivotal era of the French Renaissance and the Italian Wars. His career, spanning the late 15th and early 16th centuries, exemplifies the intricate fusion of ecclesiastical power, aristocratic lineage, and political maneuvering that characterized the French monarchy's expansionist policies under kings Charles VIII and Louis XII. His elevation to the cardinalate by Pope Julius II in 1506 solidified his family's influence and provided a crucial link between the French crown and the Papal States amidst complex European power struggles.

His ecclesiastical trajectory began with his appointment as Archbishop of Auch in 1490, a significant metropolitan see in Gascony, which he held until his death. Beyond his spiritual duties, Trémoille served as a key diplomatic agent for the French kings, particularly Louis XII. He undertook several important missions to the Holy See, advocating for French interests and negotiating alliances during a period marked by shifting loyalties and military campaigns in Italy. His efforts contributed to securing papal favor and recognition for French territorial claims and political objectives, including the control of Milan and Naples.

Trémoille's cardinalate, bestowed during the consistory of 18 December 1506, was a testament to his diplomatic skill and the strategic importance of his family to the French monarchy's foreign policy. Although his tenure as cardinal was brief, ending with his death less than a year later on June 29, 1507, his elevation underscored the close relationship between the French court and the papacy, and the use of ecclesiastical titles to cement political alliances. His position also provided a channel for French influence within the College of Cardinals, a critical body in papal elections and church governance."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T06:53:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29312614555", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1465, France"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1465, France"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Italian Wars (1494–1559) necessitated skilled diplomats to manage alliances and French interests in the Papal States and beyond.", "The powerful House of La Trémoille's aristocratic lineage and influence provided a foundation for Jean-François's rapid ascent in both church and state.", "The French monarchy's desire to exert greater influence within the College of Cardinals led to the strategic promotion of loyal French prelates like Trémoille."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T06:53:43Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29312614555", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1507-06-29"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1507-06-29"}], "newValue": ["Jean-François de la Trémoille's cardinalate briefly enhanced French diplomatic leverage within the H